prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a horse racing mini-game. Load 4 horse models. After the "Start Race" button is pressed, the horses run forward at random speeds, the rankings are displayed in real time, and the first horse to reach the finish line wins.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/Fox.glb. This model is a horse and contains a running (gallop) animation clip (iterate through gltf.animations to find it);
(2) Clone 4 horses (use SkeletonUtils.clone to ensure their skeletons are independent), and place them on the starting line (z=0), spaced 3 units apart along the x direction (x = -4.5, -1.5, 1.5, 4.5);
(3) Tint each horse a different color: red 0xff3333, blue 0x3333ff, green 0x33cc33, and yellow 0xffcc00. Traverse the child meshes and call material.color.set(...);
(4) Use an independent AnimationMixer for each horse to play the gallop animation;

[Track and Scene]
(5) The track is 50 meters long (along the z direction, from z=0 to z=50). Draw 4 lane lines on the ground (2.5 meters wide per lane);
(6) The starting line is at z=0 (white line), and the finish line is at z=50 (red line);
(7) Use a green plane for the ground and 0x87CEEB for the sky background;
(8) Use OrbitControls for the camera. The initial viewpoint must be from the side and elevated above the track, allowing the player to see the entire track;

[Race Logic]
(9) Display a "Start Race" button (id="startBtn") in the center of the page. After it is clicked, hide the button and start the race;
(10) Randomly generate each horse's speed at the start of the race (between 3-7 m/s). The speed may fluctuate slightly during the race (±0.5 m/s sinusoidal fluctuation);
(11) During the race, each horse moves in the positive z-axis direction and plays the gallop animation (the animation playback speed must be proportional to the movement speed);
(12) The first horse whose z coordinate reaches 50 wins. At that point, all horses stop moving, all animations stop, and a victory message is displayed ("Horse No. X wins!");
(13) After the race ends, display a "Race Again" button (id="restartBtn"). Clicking it returns all horses to the starting line so that another race can be run;

[Leaderboard/HUD]
(14) Display a real-time leaderboard in the upper-right corner (id="leaderboard"), sorted by each horse's z coordinate from largest to smallest, showing each rank together with the horse number and color;
(15) The HUD in the upper-left corner displays:
  - Race state (id="raceState", displaying waiting / racing / finished)
  - Elapsed time (id="elapsedTime", in seconds, rounded to 1 decimal place)
  - Winner (id="winner", displaying the winning horse number after the race ends and -1 before it ends)

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
{
  modelLoaded: boolean,       // Whether all horse models have finished loading
  raceState: string,          // 'waiting' | 'racing' | 'finished'
  horsePositions: number[],   // Array of the 4 horses' z coordinates [h1z, h2z, h3z, h4z]
  horseSpeeds: number[],      // Array of the 4 horses' current speeds
  leaderboard: number[],      // Ranking array (horse numbers, from 1st place to 4th place)
  elapsedTime: number,        // Elapsed race time (seconds)
  winner: number              // Winning horse number (1-4), or -1 before the race ends
}

Output a single HTML file, place it in the same directory as the assets/ folder, and run it through a local HTTP server.
